<tr><td>Code-required work</td><td>Drip edge, permit costs, TDI windstorm installation requirements</td><td>Code references, permit records, ordinance or law coverage</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pricing changes</td><td>Materials or labor cost more when work actually starts</td><td>Current invoices and local bids</td></tr>
<tr><td>Scope changes</td><td>Patching proves impossible because materials cannot be matched</td><td>Manufacturer information, samples, photos</td></tr>
<tr><td>Recoverable depreciation</td><td>Withheld funds released after repairs are complete</td><td>Final invoices and completion photos</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>Why Supplements Matter on Port Arthur Claims</h2>
<p>Several local conditions make supplements especially common here. Wind-driven rain from Gulf storms can soak attic insulation and wall cavities without obvious ceiling stains at first. High humidity speeds up hidden deterioration and mold once materials stay wet. Older homes in many Port Arthur neighborhoods have decades of prior repairs, multiple roof layers or materials that are no longer made. And because Jefferson County is a TWIA first-tier coastal county, roof repairs must meet TDI windstorm requirements and be certified with a WPI-8 to keep TWIA eligibility, which can change the scope compared with what the original estimate assumed.</p>
<p>Port Arthur also enforces the 2021 International Residential Code and International Building Code, and re-roofs and structural repairs generally require a city permit. Code-driven costs are only paid where the policy includes ordinance or law coverage, and only up to its limit. See our <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a> page for the details.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/photos/storm-damaged-interior-adjuster.webp" alt="Adjuster photographing storm-damaged interior walls and ceiling during repairs" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Once walls and ceilings are opened, photograph the hidden damage before anything is covered back up.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>How to Document a Supplement Correctly</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Stop and photograph.</strong> When hidden damage appears, capture wide shots, close-ups and a measuring tape for scale before the area is repaired.</li>
<li><strong>Notify the insurer.</strong> Tell the adjuster in writing what was found and offer a chance to inspect.</li>
<li><strong>Tie it to the loss.</strong> Explain how the newly found damage connects to the original cause, such as water entry at a wind-damaged roof area.</li>
<li><strong>Price it in the same format.</strong> A supplement written in Xactimate, line by line, is easier for the carrier to compare with its own estimate.</li>
<li><strong>Keep invoices and permits.</strong> Final invoices, permit records and WPI-8 certificates support both the supplement and depreciation recovery.</li>
</ol>

