<h2>Reopening Previously Settled Claims</h2>
<p>Many policyholders assume a closed claim is final. In reality, many Texas policies allow a claim to be reopened or supplemented under certain conditions, usually within a timeframe set by the policy. We review losses that were:</p>
<ul>
<li>Settled before the full extent of the damage was discovered</li>
<li>Underpaid because the scope or pricing was incomplete</li>
<li>Poorly documented, with rooms or structures missing from the estimate</li>
<li>Closed without recoverable depreciation ever being requested</li>
</ul>
<p>A closed claim may still be worth a second look if additional damage surfaced later, repairs revealed hidden issues, or the payment simply did not reflect actual repair costs. Timing is the key limitation. Policy deadlines for reporting additional damage and recovering depreciation vary, TWIA claims have their own statutory timelines, and many Texas policies set a contractual deadline to sue that may already be running. If you signed a release when the claim closed, have a Texas attorney review it. Our article on <a href="/when-is-it-too-late-to-hire-a-public-adjuster/">when it is too late to hire a public adjuster</a> covers timing in more detail.</p>

<h2>Supplements on Split TWIA and Homeowners Claims</h2>
<p>For Port Arthur homes with separate wind and homeowners coverage, supplements need to go to the right insurer. Additional roof decking or interior damage caused by wind-driven rain through a damaged roof is typically a TWIA or wind-carrier item. A plumbing leak discovered while walls are open, or freeze damage, may belong under the homeowners policy instead. Sending a supplement to the wrong carrier wastes time and can create confusion in both files. When the original claim was split between insurers, we map each supplemental item to the policy most likely to cover it and keep separate documentation for each claim, so each adjuster sees only the items that belong in their file.</p>

<h2>Common Reasons Supplements Are Rejected</h2>
<ul>
<li>The hidden damage was repaired before anyone photographed it.</li>
<li>The new damage is not clearly connected to the original covered loss.</li>
<li>The request arrived after a policy deadline.</li>
<li>Code items were requested, but the policy has no ordinance or law coverage.</li>
<li>The supplement was a single lump-sum number rather than itemized.</li>
</ul>
<p>Most of these are avoidable with good documentation. If a supplement is denied on amount rather than coverage, <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">insurance appraisal</a> may be an option.</p>

<h2>How a Port Arthur Public Adjuster Handles Supplements</h2>
<p>A Port Arthur Public Adjuster reviews the original estimate and payment history, inspects the property, documents what was missed or found later and prepares an itemized supplement for the insurer. On reopened claims, we also check the policy deadlines first so time is not wasted on a request the policy no longer allows. We do not perform repairs on claims we adjust, which Texas Insurance Code Chapter 4102 prohibits, but we work alongside the contractor you choose. Related pages: <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid insurance claims</a> and <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a>.</p>
<p>Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. Request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a> to find out whether a supplement or reopened claim may make sense.</p>
